| **Location** | Shiba‑koen, Minato‑ku, Tokyo, Japan (near Shiba‑Park and Zojo‑ji Temple) |
| **Height** | 333 m (1,093 ft) – the tallest self‑supporting steel tower in the world (taller than the Eiffel Tower) |
| **Construction** | • Built in 1958 (opened 23 Dec 1958) <br>• Design inspired by the Eiffel Tower, painted “International Orange” for air‑traffic safety |
| **Primary purpose** | • Radio & TV broadcasting (now digital & 5G) <br>• Tourist attraction with two public observation decks |
| **Observation decks** | **Main Deck** – 150 m (≈ 492 ft) – 360° city views, souvenir shop, cafés, and a small museum. <br>**Special Observatory** – 250 m (≈ 820 ft) – higher‑altitude vistas, glass‑floor sections, and a “Sky Deck” that lets you look straight down to the streets. |
| **Opening hours (2024)** | • Main Deck: 9 am – 11 pm (last entry 10:30 pm) <br>• Special Observatory: 9 am – 10:30 pm (last entry 10 pm) <br>*Hours may vary on holidays or for special events.* |
| **Admission (adult, 2024)** | • Main Deck: ¥1,200 <br>• Special Observatory: ¥3,000 <br>• Combined ticket (both decks): ¥3,200 <br>• Children (7‑11 yr): ¥700 (Main) / ¥2,000 (Special) <br>• Seniors (65+ yr): ¥800 (Main) / ¥2,500 (Special) |
| **Ticket options** | • **Day‑time ticket** – standard entry. <br>• **Night‑time ticket** – entry after sunset; the tower’s illumination makes the city sparkle. <br>• **Fast‑Pass** – skip‑the‑line tickets (available via the official website or travel agents). |
| **Dining & shopping** | • **FootTown** (four‑storey building at the base) – restaurants, snack stalls, souvenir shops, and a small aquarium. <br>• **Sky Restaurant 634** is in the nearby Tokyo Skytree, but many visitors combine a tower visit with a Skytree dinner. |
| **Nearby attractions** | • **Zojo‑ji Temple** (historic Buddhist temple, 5‑min walk) <br>• **Shiba‑Park** (green space, great for picnics) <br>• **Roppongi Hills** & **Tokyo Midtown** (art museums, shopping, nightlife) <br>• **Tokyo Tower Aquarium** (inside FootTown) |
| **Access** | • **Metro** – Oedo Line, **Akabanebashi Station** (≈ 2‑min walk) <br>• **JR Yamanote Line** – **Hamamatsucho Station** (≈ 15‑min walk or 5‑min taxi) <br>• **Toei Bus** routes 03, 04, 06 stop at “Tokyo Tower”. |
| **Lighting** | The tower’s illumination changes for special occasions: <br>• **Cherry‑blossom season** – pink hue <br>• **National holidays** – Japanese flag colors <br>• **Sports events** – team colors or sponsor logos <br>• **Seasonal themes** – e.g., “Winter Wonderland” blue lighting |
| **Fun facts** | • The antennae originally served analog TV; they now host digital broadcasting and 5G equipment. <br>• It was Japan’s tallest man‑made structure until the Tokyo Skytree (634 m) opened in 2012. <br>• The “International Orange” paint is the same color used on the Golden Gate Bridge. |
| **Official website** | https://www.tokyotower.co.jp/en/ |
| **Best time to visit** | • **Sunset** – watch the city transition from daylight to night. <br>• **Weekdays (early morning)** – fewer crowds. <br>• **Winter evenings** – the illumination is especially vivid against a clear night sky. |

### Quick Planning Tips
1. **Buy tickets ahead** – especially night‑time tickets or during peak seasons (cherry‑blossom, Golden Week, New Year). E‑tickets from the official site can be scanned at the gate.  
2. **Combine with nearby sights** – a typical itinerary: start at Tokyo Tower (morning), stroll to Zojo‑ji Temple, lunch in Roppongi, then return for a night‑time tower visit to see the lights.  
3. **Try the Special Observatory** – the glass‑floor section offers a thrilling “look‑down” experience.  
4. **Check the illumination calendar** – the website posts a schedule of lighting themes; you can time your visit to see a particular show.  
5. **Accessibility** – elevators serve both decks; wheelchair‑friendly restrooms and assistance staff are available.

